WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.770
This is actually very real.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.488
A lot of the frustration
that American soldiers

00:00:11.488 --> 00:00:14.280
experience in Vietnam
when they're on patrol

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.070
is they get hit, but they
cannot see the enemy.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.180
My name is Bill Allison.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.705
I'm a professor of history at
Georgia Southern University.

00:00:20.705 --> 00:00:22.890
I've been a battlefield
tour guide in Vietnam,

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.320
and also I've written several
books on the Vietnam War,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.180
including one on the Mỹ Lai massacre.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.280
I'm back to look at some
more Vietnam War movies

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.030
and judge how real they are.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.753
So, this is depicting the 101st
Airborne Division's assault

00:00:42.753 --> 00:00:47.753
on Hill 937 in the A Sầu Valley in 1969.

00:00:47.753 --> 00:00:49.920
Became known as Hamburger Hill.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.600
Now, the problem with this particular hill

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.130
is it's pretty steep.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.200
And because of its steepness

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.441
and that the PAVN guys

00:00:56.441 --> 00:00:59.824
are well entrenched on the top and dug in

00:00:59.824 --> 00:01:03.683
and also along the edges
of it on the slopes,

00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:07.958
it made it difficult for
aircraft to get in and hit it.

00:01:07.958 --> 00:01:10.320
Now, the strategic value
of the hill itself,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.470
it's not the physical hill.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.190
It is the fact that that
PAVN regiment is on it.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.320
One of the strategies employed in the war

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.590
by MACV, Military
Assistance Command, Vietnam,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.588
was body count, attrition.

00:01:26.588 --> 00:01:28.528
"The more we kill of them, the better."

00:01:28.528 --> 00:01:30.145
Attrition was just one

00:01:30.145 --> 00:01:33.180
of several strategic
approaches we employed.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.027
Pacification was one.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.231
Actually, this is very akin
to a World War I battle

00:01:42.231 --> 00:01:43.860
with entrenched positions.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.810
They've got machine guns set up.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.790
They've also, of course,
because they're already up there

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.819
and dug in, they've got
mortar sites pre-sighted.

00:01:51.819 --> 00:01:55.350
So as the airborne guys
are coming up the hill,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.638
they're getting popped

00:01:57.638 --> 00:02:00.813
because they're in places
that have been pre-sighted.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.940
Booby traps are synonymous with Vietnam.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.370
The VC, the Viet Cong,
especially used them

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.630
all across South Vietnam in
areas where they knew American

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.240
or ARVN soldiers might be patrolling.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.060
One of the most gruesome ones is this —

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.190
they dig a hole and they put in two rotors

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.840
with punji stakes in it.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.780
And as your leg, your
foot goes down in it,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.170
it rolls down and it chews your calf up.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.400
Pretty gruesome stuff.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.810
The PAVN's got more equipment,

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.300
so they can do more explosive-type things.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.245
They can set up more

00:02:38.245 --> 00:02:40.890
Claymore-, trip-wire-type
things, et cetera,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.560
because they have more of that
equipment available to them.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.405
So that's kind of why, in this case,

00:02:46.405 --> 00:02:50.780
you've got more of the
explosive-type stuff set up

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.622
than the punji stakes.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.240
The airborne troops did
ultimately take the hill

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.670
after several frontal assaults.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.930
Why keep doing it?

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.368
Why keep at it?

00:03:09.368 --> 00:03:12.148
In part because the
PAVN guys were up there,

00:03:12.148 --> 00:03:15.700
and I think the blood calculus,
the butcher's bill, was,

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.110
"We are doing equal if
not more harm to them."

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.360
And that was worth the cost
according to the commanders.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.170
This is fighting at its grimmest.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.272
72 Americans were killed
after the battle was over.

00:03:28.272 --> 00:03:32.220
Looking around, they found
over 600 PAVN bodies.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.862
The controversy around this
battle was the casualties,

00:03:36.862 --> 00:03:38.820
plus, days later,

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.997
the Army abandoned the
hill after it was taken.

00:03:41.997 --> 00:03:45.400
And that raised a lot of
concern in the United States

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.838
about, hey, what are we doing?

00:03:46.838 --> 00:03:48.000
I like "Hamburger Hill."

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.610
It's one of my favorite Vietnam films.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.050
And so I'm a little biased here,

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.540
but I would give this probably
eight or nine out of 10.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.893
I think this is good stuff.

00:04:02.364 --> 00:04:04.446
OK. Challenge flag.

00:04:04.446 --> 00:04:07.169
With that helicopter coming
in and staying stationary

00:04:07.169 --> 00:04:10.177
for so long in that valley

00:04:10.177 --> 00:04:12.935
in direct fire, right?

00:04:12.935 --> 00:04:13.800
They would not have done that.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.150
There would've been covering
birds, helicopters around

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:19.890
to assist if needed.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:23.100
Because if you stay still,
somebody with a, I don't know,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.533
a rocket-propelled grenade,
or an RPG, as they call it,

00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:28.563
is going to swack that helicopter.

00:04:31.932 --> 00:04:33.690
And sure enough, that's what happens.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.000
You know they're Viet Cong.
They're not uniformed.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.940
That's how you can tell. PAVN
would always have a uniform.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.680
Women serving with the Viet Cong,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.030
yes, absolutely.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.613
Gender did not matter in
this war for the Viet Cong

00:04:45.613 --> 00:04:48.060
or the PAVN, the North Vietnamese.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.041
For them, this is an existential threat.

00:04:51.041 --> 00:04:52.743
They're fighting for their survival.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.170
One thing that's good
here is the VC withdraw,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:02.790
because they must realize

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.051
that air support's going to be called in

00:05:06.051 --> 00:05:08.190
to support the guys on the ground now.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.548
And now you've got two sets
of wreckage to deal with.

00:05:11.548 --> 00:05:13.520
So they gotta get the heck out of there.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.110
The whole premise of the film,
of Spike Lee's movie here

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.720
is to show the experience

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.541
of African American soldiers in Vietnam

00:05:24.541 --> 00:05:28.136
both as combatants, but
also as veterans of the war

00:05:28.136 --> 00:05:29.370
returning to Vietnam.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.440
At least 300,000 if not more

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.200
African Americans served in the war.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.142
In the early years of the
conflict, especially '65, '66,

00:05:38.142 --> 00:05:41.880
African Americans were in combat units

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.457
and also casualties at a
rate, a percentage much higher

00:05:46.457 --> 00:05:48.131
than their percentage of the population

00:05:48.131 --> 00:05:49.389
in the United States.

00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:52.368
And this did not go unnoticed.

00:05:52.368 --> 00:05:53.790
People like Martin Luther King

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.521
and others began speaking out on this.

00:05:56.521 --> 00:06:00.660
And by 1968, '69, this had
actually kind of leveled out.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.269
I'd give it about a five, maybe a six.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.040
This is actually very real.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.360
Ambushes are very common.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.098
The Viet Cong especially used the ambush,

00:06:15.098 --> 00:06:17.730
in part because they don't
want to be exposed in the open,

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.470
because if they get
caught out in the open,

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:20.370
they're going to get hit with

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.194
the massive American firepower.

00:06:22.194 --> 00:06:24.330
What they do is try to catch the Americans

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.910
or the ARVN soldiers out on patrol.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.100
The VC would've been concealed.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.230
And in this clip, you don't see them.

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.060
That would not be that unusual.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.254
In fact, a lot of the frustration

00:06:35.254 --> 00:06:38.780
that American soldiers
experience in Vietnam

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:40.996
when they're on patrols out in the jungle

00:06:40.996 --> 00:06:42.287
or in the mountains

00:06:42.287 --> 00:06:45.003
is they get hit, but they
cannot see the enemy.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.330
You can see that the enemy
has them pre-sighted.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.810
They've got their
mortars, RPGs pre-ranged.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:58.800
That's why the Americans,
Forrest Gump's people,

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.782
have prearranged lines for
themselves to fall back to,

00:07:02.782 --> 00:07:04.320
what they're calling the "blue line."

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.931
And usually that's some
sort of physical structure.

00:07:07.931 --> 00:07:10.020
It could be a ridge, it
could be a tree line,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:13.290
it could be a canal, a
road, anything like that.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.690
All that is spot-on, absolutely.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.820
I just wish Forrest would
put his helmet back on.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.550
How many times someone gets

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:22.320
ricocheted off the side of
the head with their helmet on,

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.758
and if you don't have that
helmet on, that's all she wrote.

00:07:26.758 --> 00:07:28.716
Pull back! Pull back!

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.915
In this particular scene,
they do need to pull back.

00:07:32.915 --> 00:07:36.300
They're exposed on this sort
of road, embankment thing.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.920
They don't have a lot of cover.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.402
Dan: I got an airstrike inbound right now.

00:07:39.402 --> 00:07:41.004
They're going to nape the whole area.

00:07:42.179 --> 00:07:44.780
Calling in an airstrike.
This is very common.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:47.985
One of the big premises for the
Americans in the Vietnam War

00:07:47.985 --> 00:07:50.070
was to use the massive firepower

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:51.510
that they had at their disposal.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.655
You gotta remember, this is
a military that's equipped

00:07:53.655 --> 00:07:56.680
and trained to fight the
Russians in Western Europe.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.020
Fighting in Southeast Asia,

00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.940
they brought some of
those same ideas there.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:01.980
They didn't do it all that way,

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.500
but certainly firepower
was incredibly important.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:08.190
The problem is, the VC
are good at melting away.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:11.460
Once contact is made, they
fire a bunch of shots,

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.670
they melt away because they've
learned that the airstrike

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.558
or the artillery strike is coming.

00:08:16.558 --> 00:08:19.295
I gotta find Bubba!

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.720
So, now he's going back to rescue Bubba.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.090
And this is actually based upon an actual

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:28.620
Medal of Honor citation.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.723
Sam Davis received the Medal of Honor

00:08:30.723 --> 00:08:34.770
for going back to retrieve
actually three wounded comrades.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.900
So if you rate this on a 10 scale,

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:38.940
I would say this one's probably an eight.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:39.940
This is pretty good.

00:08:44.919 --> 00:08:49.380
What you have here in this
scene is American POWs

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.510
being held by the Viet Cong.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.210
The bamboo cages are actually for the VC

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:57.144
because that's what
they've got to work with.

00:08:57.144 --> 00:08:59.883
They don't have steel bars lying around.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.273
The Americans were
mistreated. It wasn't great.

00:09:07.273 --> 00:09:10.427
But both North Vietnam and
the Viet Cong in the South,

00:09:10.427 --> 00:09:12.450
the NLF, the National Liberation Front,

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.970
their leadership recognized
the propaganda value

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.590
of American prisoners of war.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:19.473
So you don't want to
mistreat them too much.

00:09:19.473 --> 00:09:22.874
In North Vietnam, the famous Hanoi Hilton,

00:09:22.874 --> 00:09:25.260
that's where most of the
propaganda footage is filmed

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.907
about them being in "church"
and doing a Christmas tree

00:09:27.907 --> 00:09:29.758
and doing things like that

00:09:29.758 --> 00:09:32.845
to show that they're being
treated well and whatnot,

00:09:32.845 --> 00:09:34.153
even though some of them had been there

00:09:34.153 --> 00:09:36.300
for six or seven years by that point.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.966
The South Vietnamese, they
ran the POW camps, torture.

00:09:39.966 --> 00:09:42.252
The Vietnamese police, national police,

00:09:42.252 --> 00:09:44.910
especially in Saigon, Gen. Loan,

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:47.340
I mean, they tortured, assassinated.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.472
Of course, we had the
CIA's Phoenix Program.

00:09:50.472 --> 00:09:52.587
We're doing a lot of bad things here,

00:09:52.587 --> 00:09:54.900
and a lot of bad things are
happening under our watch.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:56.899
For this, these guys are rogue.

00:09:56.899 --> 00:09:59.025
The leadership would
not be happy with this,

00:09:59.025 --> 00:10:00.007
put it that way.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.750
This particular thing is a
little outside the realm,

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:11.970
this scene depicting the VC

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.430
making the POWs play Russian roulette.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.620
There is no evidence that
that occurred at all.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.500
And obviously the way they're
depicting the Viet Cong there

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.875
is very brutal, barbaric,

00:10:23.875 --> 00:10:25.050
incredibly racist.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.691
Why? Well, again, we're
talking the late '70s.

00:10:27.691 --> 00:10:29.285
The war is still very raw.

00:10:29.285 --> 00:10:31.020
We want to still portray it

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.258
as that they were a dishonorable enemy.

00:10:33.258 --> 00:10:37.710
So to rate this on reality,
pretty low, I think.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.890
I mean, it's got to be a three or four.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.015
I don't think it's in
the realm of possibility.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.223
Nighttime attack, very common.

00:10:49.223 --> 00:10:50.730
Especially during the Tết Offensive,

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:54.060
the attacks were at night on
the 30th, 31st of January,

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.603
which is the big North
Vietnamese-orchestrated attack

00:10:57.603 --> 00:11:00.600
across all of South Vietnam.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.910
They hit almost every provincial capital.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:06.253
The problem was, in
order to undertake this,

00:11:06.253 --> 00:11:10.230
VC units and PAVN regiments
have to get out in the open.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.650
They're exposed again to
what? American firepower.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.110
And they get swacked mercilessly.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.093
The Viet Cong are never
the same again after this.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:24.405
So, the positions there,
that's all very realistic.

00:11:24.405 --> 00:11:28.161
You would've had prearranged
defensive positions, trenches,

00:11:28.161 --> 00:11:32.261
bunkers with machine guns
usually already set up

00:11:32.261 --> 00:11:34.683
as part of your perimeter defense.

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:42.510
So, the PAVN attack
where they should attack,

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:46.042
which is the main gate. That's
the best place to get in.

00:11:46.042 --> 00:11:48.090
But the problem is it also might be

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:49.410
the most heavily defended.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:52.320
It's the gate that the
Marines have to use, right?

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.500
So they can't have it mined
or that sort of thing.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.800
So that's actually in some
ways a weak spot to get in.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.105
So it's kind of a weird, I don't know,

00:12:01.105 --> 00:12:02.625
dilemma there for the enemy,

00:12:02.625 --> 00:12:04.775
but that's where they
choose to attack here.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.215
Very typical of the PAVN and the VC,

00:12:11.215 --> 00:12:12.870
mass wave assaults.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.992
Support for the war in the
United States had been falling

00:12:14.992 --> 00:12:16.470
in the fall of 1967.

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:20.400
You have the huge anti-war,
anti-draft protest

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.080
in Washington, DC.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.272
That was a big signal to
the Johnson administration

00:12:24.272 --> 00:12:26.449
that support for the war
was starting to erode.

00:12:26.449 --> 00:12:29.038
So he calls Westmoreland,
the American commander

00:12:29.038 --> 00:12:31.499
of the Military Assistance
Command, Vietnam,

00:12:31.499 --> 00:12:33.596
or MACV, as it's called, back over

00:12:33.596 --> 00:12:35.689
to give a bunch of talks on the TV shows

00:12:35.689 --> 00:12:37.597
and to Congress and whatnot

00:12:37.597 --> 00:12:39.660
to show that, no, we're
actually making progress.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.640
So support for the war actually goes up.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:44.972
Public-opinion polls
rebound a little bit, right?

00:12:44.972 --> 00:12:46.170
And then Tết happens.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:47.610
The American people are kind of like,

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:50.040
"Well, wait a minute, you
told us we were winning.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.420
That doesn't look like winning."

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:52.950
This particular scene I like a lot.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.187
I think it's pretty realistic.

00:12:55.187 --> 00:12:58.350
The nighttime fighting,
the assault on the gate,

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:01.140
the entrenched positions

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.956
for the defense of the
perimeter, et cetera.

00:13:03.956 --> 00:13:05.973
I'd give this a seven.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:14.580
Guarding bridges, very common.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:17.762
You often had US, ARVN forces

00:13:17.762 --> 00:13:20.179
on either side of a bridge over a river

00:13:20.179 --> 00:13:23.427
or a railroad bridge to protect it.

00:13:23.427 --> 00:13:25.050
Unless you wanted that bridge destroyed.

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:26.983
I'm not sure you could
be much more exposed

00:13:26.983 --> 00:13:28.650
than being on a railroad bridge.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.240
This is based on a real incident.

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:33.150
A unit murdered a young woman

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:35.010
who they had kidnapped and raped.

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:37.270
Unfortunately in Vietnam, as is the case

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:39.780
in pretty much all wars,

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.690
soldiers do mistreat noncombatants.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:44.670
There are a few units that were notorious

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:46.720
for their treatment of noncombatants.

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.610
And right on cue, here
comes your air power,

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.280
which, I'm not sure those
explosions would've been that way.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.853
Now, there you see an
American river patrol boat

00:14:02.853 --> 00:14:06.019
gets in the way and gets hit

00:14:06.019 --> 00:14:09.085
by collateral damage there.

00:14:09.085 --> 00:14:12.660
That would've been pretty
common for a river patrol boat.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:15.575
We had what we called riverine forces.

00:14:15.575 --> 00:14:17.400
So these patrol boats
would've been going up

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.080
and down the major waterways.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:20.730
I don't think it's the best duty to have

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:23.291
because you're out there
and you're exposed.

00:14:23.291 --> 00:14:25.050
Now, these things are armored-plated.

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:26.220
They've got some protection,

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:28.800
but you're really exposed out there,

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:30.905
open to ambush from
either side of the river

00:14:30.905 --> 00:14:33.780
and things like that. So it
was pretty dangerous duty.

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:38.190
I gotta rate this one probably a four,

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:41.607
because there's just so much
of it that's just not right.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:51.644
So, we've got a woman Viet Cong fighter

00:14:51.644 --> 00:14:54.152
using a tunnel. All very good.

00:14:54.152 --> 00:14:56.450
So, for the Vietcong to have

00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:00.133
tunnels and dugouts,

00:15:00.133 --> 00:15:02.378
trapdoors and things, very common.

00:15:02.378 --> 00:15:03.694
When you go to Củ Chi today,

00:15:03.694 --> 00:15:05.648
you can actually go down
in some of the tunnels,

00:15:05.648 --> 00:15:07.498
the actual, real tunnels.

00:15:07.498 --> 00:15:10.340
And there's a few places
where they have the trapdoors.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:19.890
Now that's pretty crafty.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:21.960
I don't know if they actually
had those little holes

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:23.694
they could slide a grenade through or not,

00:15:23.694 --> 00:15:24.793
push through their rifle.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:32.850
OK, she was just running
around dodging gunfire,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.923
and then she stops and just stands there.

00:15:36.602 --> 00:15:38.035
It's like, get down!

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:45.420
What they're doing is
they're hiding the weapons

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:46.710
so that when they do come through,

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.060
they don't find anything on them

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:49.290
and they can just play innocent.

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:52.073
"Hey, we're just villagers here," right?

00:15:52.073 --> 00:15:52.998
You know, "Wasn't us."

00:15:52.998 --> 00:15:54.285
You gotta remember, when this was made,

00:15:54.285 --> 00:15:56.268
in the late '70s in Vietnam,

00:15:56.268 --> 00:15:59.909
this is really promoting
still trying to —

00:15:59.909 --> 00:16:03.154
you gotta show some unity
between north and south.

00:16:03.154 --> 00:16:05.400
And of course the VC were in the south,

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:10.204
so you kind of want to
resurrect their role in the war

00:16:10.204 --> 00:16:11.640
as part of the national narrative

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.686
for the purpose of unification.

00:16:14.686 --> 00:16:17.940
Just the way "The Smell of Burning Grass,"

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.113
that film, that Vietnamese
film made more recently,

00:16:21.113 --> 00:16:23.220
kind of serves a similar purpose.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.500
Vietnam is a young country now.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.060
I mean, I think 60-something percent

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.370
of the population is under the age of 40.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:32.114
So they don't have a
living memory of the war,

00:16:32.114 --> 00:16:34.830
but you want to keep that
memory alive somehow.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.271
So how do you do this? Well,
you do it through film.

00:16:37.271 --> 00:16:39.352
I like this film.

00:16:39.352 --> 00:16:41.880
It's, again, when it was made

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:44.310
and the message it's trying to get across,

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.528
its purpose of promoting unification

00:16:46.528 --> 00:16:49.680
by kind of resurrecting the
Viet Cong role in the war,

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:52.380
it's not perfect, but
I'd give it a good six.

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:55.530
So, I have two favorite Vietnam films.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:17:00.000
One is from the early '70s
called "Go Tell the Spartans."

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.320
And it's early in the war.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:02.730
It's in the advisory phase.

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:06.475
And it's kind of foreshadowing

00:17:06.475 --> 00:17:09.231
the problems that are going to arise.

00:17:09.231 --> 00:17:11.137
The other one I like a lot is actually

00:17:11.137 --> 00:17:12.600
"Good Morning, Vietnam."

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:14.070
Thanks so much for watching.

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:16.545
Please click on the next
video; watch some more.

